> ## Summary: Macroeconomic and fiscal risks to economic stability and growth
> 
> Under the report’s high-emissions baseline, consistent with SSP3-7.0 and assuming no further adaptation and resilience, climate change is projected to create substantial and uneven macroeconomic and fiscal risks by 2050. GDP per capita remains a vital indicator because it captures consumption, investment and exports; predicts government revenues; and remains closely linked to life expectancy and other measures of well-being in lower-income countries. However, it reflects only some damaging market consequences of climate change and omits important non-market effects on health, human capital, ecosystems, communities and cultures (Stern et al., 2022). Welfare, inclusive wealth and capital measures therefore provide essential complementary evidence.
> 
> ### GDP per capita and capital
> 
> The report’s meta-analysis of **85 econometric models from 15 macroeconomic studies** projects that, by 2050, the average person lives in a country that is **8% (3–15%) poorer** in GDP-per-capita terms than it would have been without climate change. This estimate covers temperature changes, sea-level rise and some global tipping points, with additional adjustments for coastal storm surges and trade spillovers (Dietz et al., 2021; Depsky et al., 2023; Rising, 2023). Losses are substantially larger in low- and lower-middle-income countries (LICs and LMICs), at **12% (8–18%)**, and are greatest across low latitudes, particularly in Sub-Saharan Africa and Southeast Asia, where some countries are expected to lose around 20% of GDP on average.
> 
> Climate change reinforces existing inequalities: low-income countries are estimated already to be **7% (4–12%) poorer** because of climate change, rising to **12% (8–21%) poorer in 2050** and **17% (11–27%) poorer in 2075**. Because losses are concentrated in poorer and more populous countries, global GDP is projected to be only **4% (1–11%) lower** in 2050—around half the loss experienced by the average person. Under a low-emissions pathway consistent with SSP1-2.6, GDP impacts stabilise by mid-century in almost all regions.
> 
> These estimates are incomplete. They do not fully capture non-market welfare losses; slow-onset impacts other than sea-level rise; water supply and demand effects; extreme precipitation, floods, droughts and wildfires; regional tipping points such as AMOC; future tropical-cyclone changes; or the broader future undermining of economies beyond the historical record. These omissions, especially the limited representation of extreme events, imply that the estimates may be significant underestimates.
> 
> Climate change also reduces the capital base. Produced-capital losses are driven especially by acute shocks, physical depreciation and sea-level rise, while natural-capital losses weaken the ecosystem services underpinning agriculture, fisheries, tourism and resilience. Human capital is harmed through mortality, disrupted schooling, displacement, mental-health effects and impaired cognitive performance (Caruso et al., 2024; Sabarwal et al., 2024; Graff Zivin et al., 2018). These losses can generate feedback loops: weaker economic activity reduces investment, lowering the productive capital base and widening the gap in future economic potential.
> 
> ### Welfare, productivity and other macroeconomic outcomes
> 
> Total welfare losses are expected to be higher than GDP-per-capita losses. Applying the Howard and Sterner (2025) meta-analysis of **105 damage estimates from 38 studies**, the report projects total climate damages in 2050 equivalent to **14% (8–19%) of global GDP per capita**. For LICs and LMICs, median welfare losses reach **16% (8–23%)**. These GDP-equivalent losses represent the consumption reduction that would produce the same welfare loss as the full range of market and non-market climate impacts. Almost half of projected damages are attributable to catastrophic impacts, while around one-third arises from effects on economic growth rates rather than immediate annual losses. Under low emissions, damages in 2050 are equivalent to 11% of GDP and stabilise below 12%; under the high-emissions baseline, they rise to 22% in 2075.
> 
> A separate synthesis of **eight labour-productivity studies** finds a global reduction in labour productivity of **0.6% (0.0–0.7%)** by 2050, increasing to **1.4% (0.0–2.8%)** in LICs and LMICs. Some parts of Africa and Asia experience losses above 5%. Heat stress is particularly consequential in economies reliant on outdoor and weather-exposed work, although high-income economies also face substantial absolute losses.
> 
> Combining **three total-factor-productivity (TFP) studies**, the report estimates a **0.03 percentage-point-per-year** reduction in TFP growth globally and in LICs/LMICs. The largest mean reductions occur in Africa, and uncertainty is substantial: a one-in-four chance of TFP being at least 0.3 percentage points lower per year across much of the world would imply a 7% TFP loss by 2050.
> 
> A synthesis of **two spatially differentiated studies** projects that inflation rates rise by **1.6 percentage points per year globally** and **1.7 percentage points per year in LICs and LMICs** in 2050, with the largest increases across Africa, the Middle East and South Asia (Kotz et al., 2024; Boirard et al., 2022). Food-price increases are a principal mechanism, alongside energy shortages, supply-chain disruption, reduced labour productivity and capital loss.
> 
> The report re-projects estimates from Liu and Lin (2023), finding highly heterogeneous unemployment effects: a global change of **−0.23 percentage points** and an LIC/LMIC change of **0.1 percentage points** in 2050. Warming tends to raise unemployment in countries between 20° and 40° north and south, while effects may differ in cooler regions. Syntheses of eight macro-models project an increase in the Gini index of **0.2 points globally** and **0.3 points in LICs and LMICs**, reflecting disproportionate impacts on vulnerable households (Emmerling et al., 2024).
> 
> ### Fiscal and sovereign-credit risks
> 
> Applying Klusak et al. (2023) to 2050, the report projects climate-driven sovereign-credit-rating reductions of **1.4 notches globally** and **1.0 notch in LICs and LMICs**. Downgrades raise borrowing costs and can constrain both recovery spending and investment in resilience. Although the largest projected rating changes occur in upper-middle- and high-income countries—whose higher initial ratings leave further to fall—climate-vulnerable poorer countries already face measurable debt-market risk premiums and constrained fiscal capacity (Kling et al., 2018; Cevik and Jalles, 2022).
> 
> The evidence review indicates a broad fiscal squeeze: climate change tends to increase public expenditure while reducing revenues. Yet observed effects on public debt remain uncertain, partly because governments may respond to climate shocks by reducing expenditure rather than increasing borrowing. This can slow recovery and deepen long-term vulnerability. Adaptation is also systematically under-recognised in credit assessments, despite evidence that adaptive capacity affects sovereign-credit outcomes. Better integration of climate risks and adaptation into debt-sustainability analysis, resilience bonds, contingent credit lines and multilateral support could help break the cycle in which higher borrowing costs reduce the ability to finance resilience.
