> ## Summary: The case for adaptation investment
> 
> Strategic investments in adaptation and resilience are increasingly urgent for economic decision-makers, particularly Ministries of Finance, as climate impacts escalate. The core adaptation dividend is **damage reduction**: adaptation can reduce losses in agriculture, infrastructure and public health before they become severe economic disruption. It includes both **reactive adaptation**, which addresses risks already being experienced and helps contain loss and damage, and **proactive adaptation**, which anticipates future climate risks and reduces future disruption and damages (Carleton et al., 2024). Existing macroeconomic and fiscal frameworks generally treat climate shocks as downside risks but do not incorporate the full upside potential of resilience investments (IMF, 2025; Fitch Ratings, 2023). The evidence reviewed here helps correct that asymmetry.
> 
> This section reviews adaptation costs, benefits and economic returns from **75 studies**, and synthesises results from **22 studies** into three complementary metrics: the **adaptation effectiveness ratio** (AER), the **economic benefit–cost ratio** (BCR) and the **economic internal rate of return** (EIRR). The evidence combines ex-ante engineering and modelling estimates, statistical analyses of observed returns, and economy-wide modelling. Most studies focus on physical adaptation measures, particularly infrastructure; evidence on non-technical, nature-based and social-protection approaches remains more limited. Results are site- and context-specific, reflecting local hazards, exposure, vulnerability and existing protection.
> 
> The AER is the share of baseline expected losses avoided because an adaptation measure is in place:
> 
> \[
> \mathrm{AER} =
> \frac{\text{baseline expected losses} - \text{expected losses with adaptation}}
> {\text{baseline expected losses}}.
> \]
> 
> The literature reviewed has a bimodal distribution: some broadly defined, ex-post interventions show small effects across large populations (AER below 0.05), while project-specific, ex-ante estimates often show much larger benefits (AER above 0.10). Locally targeted adaptation can reduce climate risks by **38–72%** (World Bank, 2024a). Early warning systems have a conservative AER of around **0.20**, while urban heat action plans can achieve AERs of **0.4–0.8** for avoided excess mortality (Hallegatte et al., 2019; Azhar et al., 2014; Knowlton et al., 2014). Across the report’s sectoral synthesis, infrastructure has an average AER of **0.23 (0.12–0.38)**; agriculture, forestry and fisheries **0.10 (0.05–0.18)**; and human health **0.14 (0.0–0.33)**.
> 
> Economic benefit–cost ratios show whether adaptation “pays off” by comparing discounted benefits—principally avoided losses and service continuity, and sometimes health and ecosystem benefits—with discounted capital and operating costs over a project’s lifetime. The vast majority of reported BCRs in the review are between **3:1 and 8:1**, though some measures addressing extreme weather or high-value health impacts exceed 20:1. Sectoral median BCRs are **6.4 (3–13)** for extreme-weather adaptation, **5 (3–8)** for infrastructure, **4.6 (3–15)** for human health, **3.8 (3–5)** for water supply and flood protection, **3.9 (3–5)** for coastal zones, and **3.2 (2–6)** for agriculture, forestry and fisheries. High returns are particularly common for multi-hazard early warning and hydrometeorological systems, flood protection and coastal ecosystem restoration, though results depend on exposure, valuation scope, discount rates, delivery and maintenance, and the effectiveness of end-user responses.
> 
> EIRR is the discount rate at which a project’s discounted economic benefits equal its costs. All EIRR estimates in the database derive from WRI (Brandon et al., 2025). The median EIRR is around **25%**, comfortably above typical public-investment thresholds, and some disaster-risk-reduction investments achieve approximately double this value. Sectoral median EIRRs are **35% (23–66%)** for extreme-weather adaptation, **32.2% (28–42%)** for agriculture, forestry and fisheries, **26% (25–58%)** for human health, **23.2% (20–28%)** for infrastructure and **17.4% (16–21%)** for water supply and flood protection. Because this evidence focuses on early interventions that produce early benefits, accumulated benefits could exceed costs after about **three years for the median project**, if benefits begin in the year after costs are paid. These estimates include market and, where in scope, non-market benefits; financial returns alone are often substantially lower.
> 
> Taken together, the three measures present a consistent picture: adaptation not only reduces damages to society, but is also a sound economic investment, generating strong returns. The review does not find statistically significant differences between estimates for “leading projects” and estimates for countries’ total adaptation needs, nor between ex-ante and ex-post estimates, although ex-post evidence remains scarce and leading-project data may inform broader estimates.
> 
> The fiscal case is also important. Climate-related capital losses and labour-productivity declines reduce tax revenue and increase recovery expenditure, weakening fiscal balances and potentially raising borrowing costs (Bernhofen et al., 2024). Adaptation can reduce these losses and help break the resulting adverse chain of economic disruption. An econometric study finds that a 1% increase in climate vulnerability raises long-term government-bond spreads in emerging markets by **3.1%**, while a 1% increase in resilience reduces them by **0.75%** (Cevik et al., 2022). Early adoption can improve debt-to-GDP dynamics by enabling countries to restore capital stocks more rapidly after shocks and reducing credit-rating downgrades (Catalano et al., 2020; Klusak et al., 2023). However, adaptation still requires upfront public expenditure, and fiscal space is severely constrained: only **21 of 64** low-income countries are estimated to have fiscal space above 20% of GDP for adaptation without high debt-distress risk (Chamon et al., 2022). Moreover, many adaptation measures create public goods and positive externalities that private investors cannot fully capture.
> 
> Adaptation can also yield a **triple dividend**: preventing loss and damage; stimulating economic activity, innovation, jobs and productivity; and delivering social and environmental co-benefits, including improved health, biodiversity and quality of life (Surminski et al., 2016). In the WRI database of 320 ex-ante projects, co-benefits exceed avoided losses across all sectors (Brandon et al., 2025). Yet adaptation does not automatically produce a triple dividend: interventions can entail trade-offs, including environmental impacts, debt-financing costs or uneven distributions of benefits. Measures with high co-benefits but small avoided losses may leave substantial residual climate risks.
> 
> Adaptation is closely intertwined with development. Resilient infrastructure, services, social protection, formal finance, livelihood diversification, local capacity and inclusive governance can protect development gains and reduce vulnerability to both climatic and non-climatic shocks. But targeted adaptation may also compete with other development priorities, and the balance will vary by country, risk and time period. Nature-based solutions can be particularly valuable where they jointly protect natural capital, reduce climate risks, support productivity and advance mitigation—although they are not substitutes for engineered protection in all high-risk settings.
> 
> Adaptation needs in developing countries are estimated at **US$310–365 billion per year by 2035** (UNEP, 2025), with costs rising under higher emissions. Although LICs and LMICs face lower absolute costs than wealthier countries, their needs make up a larger share of GDP. Mitigation reduces these costs: by 2050, adaptation costs in developing countries are estimated at **0.27–0.89% of GDP** under high emissions, compared with **0.11–0.35%** under low emissions (Chapagain et al., 2020).
> 
> Finally, aggregating sectoral BCRs using UNEP’s region- and sector-specific adaptation investment needs produces economy-wide BCRs for developing countries of around **4:1**. This indicates that appropriately scaled adaptation investment can yield substantial economic returns, though future projects must be assessed iteratively and tailored to changing physical risks and local socioeconomic conditions. GDP alone is insufficient for this purpose: complementary measures of comprehensive wealth and ecosystem services are needed to capture the human, produced and natural capital that underpins long-term resilience, sustainability and welfare (World Bank, 2024e).
