> ### Macro risks
> 
> The projections of impacts on GDP miss or under-estimate several important risks, including regional tipping points, storms, floods, droughts, spatial spillovers and ecosystem shifts. Only **3% of reviewed sources** take climate tipping points into account, meaning that mid- and late-century impacts are likely to be underestimated. Both statistical and macromodelling approaches generally assume that the basic structure of the economy, climate stability and the economy–climate relationship remain relatively unchanged, limiting their ability to represent thresholds and systemic disruption.
> 
> Results generally present expected impacts, such as median GDP losses, but these can hide important changes in extreme shocks. An average loss can reflect either steady losses or years of relative stability followed by a devastating event. More work on stress testing against potential extreme events and multi-event scenarios is therefore needed. Current assessments also tend to emphasise marginal losses while overlooking systemic risks, such as ecosystem collapse, deterioration of social cohesion, cascading effects through supply chains and wider financial instability (Climate Financial Risk Forum, 2025).
> 
> The role of extreme events and precipitation remains understudied, and acute hazards such as floods, droughts and storms are missing from many estimates. There is also a strong evidence bias towards large countries, including the US and China; macroeconomic and fiscal risks across much of the lower-income world remain poorly studied. Physical-risk effects on unemployment, TFP, natural and human capital, and the long-term welfare consequences of capital loss require substantially more research.
> 
